description:
Implement a minimal, experimental **Wheel local session daemon** so a Wheel runtime can outlive a client and expose a local control plane.

## Objective

Add a long-lived per-user Wheel process that owns a `WheelContext`, session metadata, and a small request/response protocol. Clients can start, inspect, query/update context, snapshot, and stop it without embedding the Lua runtime in the client. This is the first slice of the Herdr/bittyd-inspired runtime boundary, not a full task executor.

## MVP protocol

Use a local POSIX FIFO control plane (pure Lua 5.1, no new dependencies, no TCP, no Unix socket library):

- request FIFO and response FIFO under a private per-session runtime directory;
- one JSON request line per call, one JSON response line per call;
- bounded request/response sizes (64 KiB request, 1 MiB response);
- protocol marker `wheel-session/1`;
- a client-side lock so concurrent CLI calls serialize;
- stale metadata/PID detection on start.

Methods:

- `ping` — protocol, session, pid, version, uptime;
- `session.status` — session metadata, context slot count/tree hash, protocol;
- `session.snapshot` — bounded context snapshot (slot names + content hashes, not secret values unless explicitly requested? choose a bounded safe shape);
- `context.put` — name/content/expected_version;
- `context.get` — name;
- `context.list` — prefix;
- `shutdown` — reply then stop the daemon.

The daemon must not claim full task/agent execution. Provider calls, task DAG ownership, and remote access are out of scope for this slice.

## Files (suggested)

- `lua/wheel/session.lua` — paths, metadata, session-name validation, JSON framing helpers, client RPC.
- `lua/wheel/daemon.lua` — server loop and method dispatch.
- `scripts/wheel-session` — POSIX shell CLI: `start`, `stop`, `status`, `snapshot`, `rpc`; `start` uses `setsid`, private `umask 077`, creates FIFOs safely, waits for readiness.
- `tests/test_session.lua` (or split unit/integration) — protocol, validation, lifecycle, security.
- `README.md` — new "Local session daemon (experimental)" section.
- `SECURITY-MODEL.md` — local-only trust boundary, same-user FIFO residual (no peer credentials), no network listener.
- `CHANGELOG.md` — Unreleased entry.

## Security requirements

- runtime dir `0700`, FIFOs/metadata `0600`; refuse symlinked/non-owned/unsafe paths;
- reject invalid session names (`[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]{0,31}`);
- no secrets, API keys, or full provider credentials in metadata/snapshot/logs;
- bounded frames and static error messages;
- stale PID handling must not kill an unrelated process;
- no `pkill`/pattern kill; signal only recorded PIDs after validating the daemon metadata;
- no TCP/remote listener and no ambient plugin authority;
- document the same-user residual honestly.

## Acceptance

- `just check` passes; new integration test starts a real daemon in a temp dir, proves context survives separate client processes, status/snapshot work, shutdown is clean, and invalid/oversized/symlink cases fail closed.
- No changes outside the task scope; no push/PR by the implementer; commit in the assigned worktree and report the commit plus evidence.
- Record progress/checkpoint in CarryCtx with the provided identity/task.

Implement the first append-only execution audit stream for Wheel, closing part of the Gate 1/Gate 2 audit gap.

## Objective

Add a local, bounded, line-delimited audit sink under `$XDG_STATE_HOME/wheel/audit/` and wire it to the process boundary so a high-risk spawn either has a pre-spawn record or fails closed.

## Requirements

- `lua/wheel/audit.lua`:
  - resolve the audit directory from an explicit test/embedding override or `XDG_STATE_HOME/wheel/audit`; never a project-controlled path;
  - create dir `0700`, files `0600`, append-only JSONL;
  - bounded records (e.g. 16 KiB), bounded rotate size/retention (e.g. 1 MiB x 4), monotonic sequence and previous-record hash chain for silent-tamper detection (not a same-user boundary);
  - static typed failures that never contain secrets; redaction/stable hash for redacted values; no prompts, file contents, raw provider responses, auth headers, or secret values;
  - `record(event, opts)` with `high_risk=true` failing closed when the pre-spawn write cannot complete.
- Wire the process boundary (`WheelHost.proc.run_program`/`run_command`) through an optional `opts.audit` metadata table:
  - pre-spawn event: record before launching, including role/agent/task/tool/intent when supplied, argv/redacted shell command, cwd, allow decision/reason, and a stable command hash;
  - completion event: exit status, duration, truncation, cleanup state;
  - if a high-risk pre-spawn audit write fails, do not spawn and return a typed failure;
  - default behavior must not surprise existing tests; make the sink explicit/opt-in for this slice, with a clear path to default-on later. Document the choice honestly.
- Add `tests/test_audit.lua` covering append/rotation/hash chain, private modes, redaction/no secrets, bounded records, high-risk fail-closed before spawn, non-high-risk warning/continue, and concurrency/append behavior where practical.
- Add a **real-host Gate 2 audit fixture** (extend `tests/real_host_fixture.lua` or a focused test) that runs a real permitted command and a denied command with audit enabled, then has the test inspect the JSONL output (not an implementer summary): pre-spawn record exists, fields present, denied command did not spawn, no secret/synthetic value, mode `0600`.
- `just test-audit` and wire into `just check`; docs: `SECURITY-MODEL.md` Gate 2/Gate 1, README audit section, CHANGELOG.
- Do not edit session daemon/snapshot, benchmark, or provider-stub files except minimal shared docs/justfile if required.

## Non-goals

Asynchronous cancellation, remote telemetry, cryptographic tamper-proofing, or a full policy engine. Audit is local and same-user-tamperable; state that residual.

## Acceptance

`just check` green; real audit record inspected by the test; high-risk spawn fails closed without a record; no secrets/paths leak; independent review + CI. Commit in the assigned worktree; do not push/PR/merge.
